---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import '../../styles/ctf.css';
import Cmd from '../../components/Cmd.astro';
import { coreCommands, extraCommands, ctfRepo } from '../../lib/termux-ctf';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/+$/, '') + '/';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How to play the Termux CTF"
  description="The four commands you need, what each one does, and a worked example of the first challenge."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="ctf-hero">
      <h1>How to play</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Four commands cover almost everything. They work the same way for every
        challenge, so once you have played one you have played them all.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section>
      <h2>The four you will use constantly</h2>
      <div class="cmd-table">
        {coreCommands.map((c) => (
          <div class="cmd-row">
            <code>{c.cmd}</code>
            <span>{c.what}</span>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p>
        Anywhere a command takes an <code>&lt;id&gt;</code>, the number and the name both
        work. <code>ctf start 01</code> and <code>ctf start hidden-file</code> do the same
        thing.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Three more, for when you need them</h2>
      <div class="cmd-table">
        {extraCommands.map((c) => (
          <div class="cmd-row">
            <code>{c.cmd}</code>
            <span>{c.what}</span>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p>
        <code>ctf hint</code> reveals one hint at a time and remembers how many you have
        seen, so asking again gives you the next one rather than starting over.
        <code>ctf doctor</code> is the first thing to run if something behaves oddly.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The menu</h2>
      <p>
        Typing on a phone is the worst part of any terminal tool, so running
        <code>ctf</code> with no arguments opens a menu instead of a wall of text. It is
        the same seven commands, numbered, and it stays open until you quit.
      </p>
      <p class="ctf-empty">
        Piped into another command, it prints the how-to-play text instead, so scripts
        never end up waiting for input that will not come.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>A whole session, start to finish</h2>
      <p>This is the first challenge, played from beginning to end.</p>

      <Cmd cmd="ctf list" note="See what is there." />

      <div class="ctf-banner">
        <pre>  ────────────────────────────────────────────────
    01   hidden-file          Easy     10 pts  open
    02   owner-only           Easy     15 pts  open
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────
    0 of 6 captured, 0 of 120 points</pre>
      </div>

      <Cmd cmd="ctf start 01" note="Read the briefing. It writes the files and tells you how many." />

      <div class="ctf-banner">
        <pre>Two files were written into your home directory before you started.

Both have names that begin with a dot. A plain listing leaves those out,
which is why a directory can look emptier than it is.

One of the two holds your flag. The other is noise.

  Ready. 2 file(s) are in place in your home directory.</pre>
      </div>

      <p>
        Now the part that is up to you. A plain <code>ls</code> shows nothing, which is the
        first half of the lesson. The listing command has a flag of its own for showing
        everything.
      </p>

      <Cmd cmd="ls -a" />
      <Cmd cmd="cat .backup-52b09ac4" note="Read the file you found. The name is different on your device." />

      <Cmd cmd="ctf check 01 LEARMUX&#123;...&#125;" note="Paste the flag you found. Copy buttons are everywhere in the tool." />

      <div class="ctf-banner">
        <pre>  LEARMUX&#123;3f2a91c4b7e05d18&#125;
  Correct. 01 captured, 10 points.

  10 of 120 points.  ctf list to see what is left.</pre>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>When you get stuck</h2>
      <ol class="ctf-steps">
        <li>
          <strong>Re-read the briefing</strong>
          <span>It teaches the concept the challenge is testing. The answer is usually a step you skipped.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Run <code>ctf start &lt;id&gt;</code> again</strong>
          <span>It prints everything a second time without touching your progress.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Ask for one hint</strong>
          <span>Then go and try again. Come back for the second one only if you need it.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Reset the challenge</strong>
          <span>If you deleted or edited something past the point where it makes sense, <code>ctf reset &lt;id&gt;</code> puts it back.</span>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        If a flag looks right and is rejected, check you copied all of it. A flag is
        exactly 25 characters: <code>LEARMUX&#123;</code>, sixteen characters, and a
        closing brace.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Where things are kept</h2>
      <p>
        The tool and your progress live in <code>~/.learmux</code>, and the command itself
        is installed into <code>$PREFIX/bin</code>. Every path the tool creates is recorded
        in a manifest as it goes, which is what lets the uninstaller remove exactly those
        paths and nothing else.
      </p>
      <Cmd cmd="./uninstall.sh" note="Removes only what the tool recorded, and prints the list first." />
      <p>
        <a class="btn" href={`${base}ctf/challenges/`}>See the challenges</a>
        <a class="btn" href={ctfRepo} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the source</a>
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
